---
import "../styles/global.css";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>About Me | DigitalWoodworker</title>
    <link
      rel="icon"
      type="image/x-icon"
      href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "favicon.ico"}
    />
    <link
      rel="shortcut icon"
      type="image/x-icon"
      href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "favicon.ico"}
    />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Learn about the creator behind DigitalWoodworker."
    />
    <script
      data-goatcounter="https://digitalwoodworker.goatcounter.com/count"
      async
      src="//gc.zgo.at/count.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body class="bg-white text-slate-900">
    <Header />
    <main class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-20">
      <section class="mb-12 text-center">
        <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.4em] text-amber-500">
          About Me
        </p>
        <h1
          class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-950 sm:text-5xl"
        >
          Meet the craftsman behind every piece.
        </h1>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-2xl text-base leading-8 text-slate-600">
          Driven by patience, uncompromised tools, and a relentless commitment
          to fine woodcraft.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div
          class="rounded-[1.75rem] border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-10 shadow-sm"
        >
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-950">
            A Passion Carved in Wood
          </h2>
          <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-8 text-slate-600">
            Hi there! I’m Nithin, the face behind DigitalWoodworker.
            <br /><br />

            I hold a Master's in Electronics and, like many graduates in the
            2000s, landed in the Indian IT industry. Crafting started as a
            childhood hobby, but over time, I took it to a professional
            level— delving into papercutting, glass engraving, and wood-resin
            art. I spent a few years on these crafts, but deep down, I realized
            they weren’t my true calling and I was still searching for something
            that truly challenged me. Then, almost by chance, I picked up
            woodworking. The moment I started shaping wood, everything clicked:
            I had finally found a medium with the permanence, character, and
            structural challenge I had been looking for—a way to build tangible
            things that leave a mark and outlive me.
            <br /><br />
            My woodworking journey started humbly with a single Dremel Moto-Saw. Back
            then, being a DIYer in India was an uphill battle; sourcing specialty
            tools, hardware, and raw materials felt like a Herculean task, and finding
            anyone else sharing the same interest was nearly impossible.
            <br /><br />
            Everything changed during the COVID lockdowns. I started an Instagram
            account to share my projects and finally connected with a vibrant community
            of fellow makers to exchange guidance and ideas. Since then, there’s been
            no turning back. Today, my work spans everything from handcrafted wooden
            boxes to custom chess sets, one-of-a-kind scale models, and extreme-scale
            micro miniatures.
            <br /><br />
            My background in electronics and IT directly influences how I approach
            every build. The "Digital" in DigitalWoodworker isn't just a label—it
            represents my precision-driven mindset. I treat woodworking as an engineering
            discipline, with a passion for tackling complex technical challenges and
            going to whatever lengths necessary to solve them. To push those limits,
            I continuously source niche, highly specialized machinery from around
            the globe—equipment that remains extremely rare in India—to bring absolute
            precision to every intricate detail.
            <br /><br />
            Follow along as I push the boundaries of scale, precision, and craft on
            the workbench, or reach out if you have an impossible woodworking challenge
            you need solved!
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </body>
</html>
